__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store
*.egg-info/
.env
venv/

# Local Netlify folder
.netlify

# Universe source layer.
#
# COMMITTED: public/universe_candidates.json and public/universe_ranking.json
# are the artifacts the API serves; data/universe_active.json is the weekly
# rotation cache signal_engine consumes; data/pool/ holds the hand-maintained
# pool inputs (Nasdaq-100 membership, inclusions).
#
# COMMITTED as of 2026-08-10: data/universe_cache/etf_*.json. These are the
# only record of WHICH holdings produced a given pool. Without them, "SPY put
# NVDA in the pool" is an inference from whatever Yahoo returns today, not
# evidence about the build that actually ran. The rotation workflow commits
# them back on every rotation, so the committed copy is always the snapshot
# the build used — committing them without that step would have been worse
# than not committing them, since a permanently stale file reads as evidence.
#
# IGNORED, and never authoritative: the data/ copies of the ranking and
# candidates artifacts. They are local build byproducts of a local rotation
# run. Rotations normally run in Actions, which commits only the public/
# copies, so a data/ copy on a developer machine is frozen at whenever that
# machine last built and must not be read as current. Nothing in production
# reads them; nothing committed should either.
# NOTE: the first pattern must exclude the directory's CONTENTS (trailing
# /*), not the directory itself — git will not descend into an excluded
# directory, and the negation below would then never be reached. For the same
# reason the negation only works while the caches stay FLAT: an
# etf_*.json nested under a subdirectory here would be ignored again.
data/universe_cache/*
!data/universe_cache/etf_*.json
data/universe_candidates.json
data/universe_ranking.json

# Build 4 backtest — raw market-data pulls are cached locally, never
# committed; the committed artifacts are the results JSON + report + charts.
data/backtest_cache/
data/doctrine_cache/

# The Flask dev server's own search log. ticker_api writes it on every
# /api/analyze and reads it back for the search page, but a missing file
# degrades to {"searches": []} by design, so it is load-bearing for nobody.
# Ignored rather than merely left untracked because update-signals.yml stages
# the WHOLE data/ directory (`git add ... data/ ...`) — without an entry here,
# one laptop's browsing record is one broad `git add` away from being committed
# as if it were pipeline data.
data/search_history.json

# Smoke twins of the study results. backtest_carry.py and backtest_target.py
# both redirect a --smoke run from <name>.json to <name>.smoke.json precisely
# so a smoke run cannot clobber the committed artifact. The redirect is the
# fix; this entry keeps its output from being read as a result. A FAMILY
# pattern, not one filename, because there are already two such scripts.
docs/*.smoke.json

# The PUBLIC mirror of the position-events log — and ONLY the public one.
#
# THE ANCHORING IS THE POINT. data/position_events.json is TRACKED and is the
# committed 74-event log the API serves; public/position_events.json is an
# untracked local-run byproduct (position_signals writes both copies). A bare
# `position_events.json` here would match the TRACKED file too, so the leading
# slash anchors this to the repo root and to the public/ copy alone.
#
# Committing the public copy is the actively harmful option, not a tidy one:
# history.html fetches the relative 'position_events.json' FIRST and labels it
# 'live run artifact', falling through to the committed log only when the
# public copy is empty. A committed local copy would therefore REPLACE the full
# history on the live page with whatever one laptop last produced.
/public/position_events.json

# Claude Code project config. .claude/launch.json is portable (repo-relative
# dev-server commands) and is committed; anything *.local.json is per-machine
# state — the permission allowlist in particular — and must never be.
.claude/*.local.json
